<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">River Trent</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trento">Trent</a> is the third-longest river in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, nearly 300 kilometres long, flowing from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/staffordshire">Staffordshire</a> moorlands through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/midlands">Midlands</a> to join the Ouse and form the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/humber">Humber</a> estuary. Its upper valley was the heartland of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxons">Anglo-Saxon</a> kingdom of Mercia, and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> it was regarded as the dividing line between northern and southern England.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/river-trent.jpg" alt="River Trent" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Andrew Abbott · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:River_Trent_-_geograph.org.uk_-_6470987.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
